      "text": "To determine the value of \\(a\\) so that the lines with the given equations are perpendicular, we need to find the slopes of both lines and use the property that the product of the slopes of two perpendicular lines is \\(-1\\).\n\nThe first line is given by the equation:\n\\[ y = 2x + 13. \\]\nFrom this equation, we can see that the slope of the first line is \\(m_1 = 2\\).\n\nNext, we need to rewrite the second line equation \\(6y + ax = 6\\) in slope-intercept form \\(y = mx + b\\). To do this, we solve for \\(y\\):\n\\[ 6y + ax = 6. \\]\nSubtract \\(ax\\) from both sides:\n\\[ 6y = -ax + 6. \\]\nDivide every term by 6:\n\\[ y = -\\frac{a}{6}x + 1. \\]\nFrom this equation, we can see that the slope of the second line is \\(m_2 = -\\frac{a}{6}\\).\n\nFor the lines to be perpendicular, the product of their slopes must be \\(-1\\):\n\\[ m_1 \\cdot m_2 = -1. \\]\nSubstitute the slopes into the equation:\n\\[ 2 \\cdot \\left(-\\frac{a}{6}\\right) = -1. \\]\nSimplify the left side:\n\\[ -\\frac{2a}{6} = -1. \\]\nThis simplifies to:\n\\[ -\\frac{a}{3} = -1. \\]\nMultiply both sides by \\(-3\\):\n\\[ a = 3. \\]\n\nTherefore, the value of \\(a\\) that makes the lines perpendicular is \\(\\boxed{3}\\).",
