      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To determine the value of \\( a \\) so that the given lines are perpendicular, we first need to find the slopes of the lines. Two lines are perpendicular if the product of their slopes is \\(-1\\).\n\nThe first equation is given in slope-intercept form \\( y = mx + b \\), where \\( m \\) is the slope. For the equation \\( y = 2x + 13 \\), the slope \\( m_1 \\) is 2.\n\nNext, we rewrite the second equation in slope-intercept form to find its slope. The equation is \\( 6y + ax = 6 \\). We solve for \\( y \\):\n\n\\[\n6y + ax = 6\n\\]\n\nSubtract \\( ax \\) from both sides:\n\n\\[\n6y = -ax + 6\n\\]\n\nDivide every term by 6:\n\n\\[\ny = -\\frac{a}{6}x + 1\n\\]\n\nThe slope \\( m_2 \\) of the second line is \\( -\\frac{a}{6} \\).\n\nFor the lines to be perpendicular, the product of their slopes must be \\(-1\\):\n\n\\[\nm_1 \\cdot m_2 = -1\n\\]\n\nSubstitute the slopes \\( m_1 = 2 \\) and \\( m_2 = -\\frac{a}{6} \\):\n\n\\[\n2 \\cdot \\left( -\\frac{a}{6} \\right) = -1\n\\]\n\nSimplify the left-hand side:\n\n\\[\n-\\frac{2a}{6} = -1\n\\]\n\nSimplify the fraction:\n\n\\[\n-\\frac{a}{3} = -1\n\\]\n\nTo solve for \\( a \\), multiply both sides by \\(-3\\):\n\n\\[\na = 3\n\\]\n\nThus, the value of \\( a \\) that makes the lines perpendicular is \\(\\boxed{3}\\).",
